course: biology
lesson: 82
title: The big cats house: snow leopards, jaguars, small cats and enrichment
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every cat in the big cats house is built for its own home: the snow leopard wraps its long furry tail round its face like a scarf, and its wide paws work like snowshoes on mountain snow.
explain: The jaguar has the strongest bite of any big cat for its size, strong enough to crack a turtle's shell, and the clouded leopard can walk down a tree trunk head first like a squirrel.
explain: Keepers know that a bored cat is an unhappy cat, so they throw in a cardboard box stuffed with hay and meat, or spray a little perfume on a log, and the tiger tears and the lion sniffs and rolls.
explain: It is just what your kitten does with a box or a ball of string, because play and searching are practice for hunting, only a tiger's ball must be huge and tough.
fact: Snow leopards live high in the mountains of Central Asia; their tail is almost as long as their body and keeps the face warm and helps them balance on cliffs.
fact: A snow leopard's wide, fur-covered paws spread its weight like snowshoes so it does not sink; it cannot roar and makes a puffing sound instead.
fact: The jaguar has the strongest bite of any big cat for its size and often kills by biting through the skull or a turtle's shell.
fact: Jaguars and leopards both have rosettes, but a jaguar's usually have small dots inside; jaguars live in the Americas and leopards in Africa and Asia.
fact: A «black panther» is a dark leopard or jaguar, not a separate kind of animal; its spots still show in bright light.
fact: Clouded leopards can climb down trees head first because their ankles turn backwards, and they have the longest canine teeth for their size of any living cat.
fact: A cheetah hunts by sight, so it likes a high spot to watch open land; it can reach about 100 kilometres per hour, but only for a few hundred metres before it overheats.
fact: Enrichment means giving animals things to search, smell, tear and solve, like boxes of hay and meat, so their day is more like life in the wild.
fact: Keepers sometimes spray perfume or spices in an enclosure, because new smells give cats something to explore, like a new toy.
fact: Pumas, also called cougars or mountain lions, chirp, hiss, purr and scream but cannot roar; a mountain lion is not a lion.
fact: There are about 40 kinds of wild cats, and most are small, like the swamp-hunting fishing cat with slightly webbed feet and the desert sand cat, whose hairy paws stop its feet burning on hot sand.
fact: Big cats play with balls, ropes and barrels, especially young ones, because play trains the moves they need for hunting.
exercise: Why does a snow leopard wrap its tail round its face? | To keep its face warm in the freezing mountains | To hide its face from the visitors who stare at it all day long | To clean its whiskers | To stop it from roaring
exercise: What else does a snow leopard's long tail help with? | Balancing when it leaps between rocks | Swimming across cold, fast mountain rivers | Digging burrows | Catching fish
exercise: How do a snow leopard's huge furry paws help it? | They work like snowshoes and stop it sinking | They let it run even faster than a cheetah on flat ground | They help it climb down trees head first | They are used to dig for water
exercise: Which big cat has the strongest bite for its size? | The jaguar | The cheetah | The puma, or cougar | The snow leopard
exercise: How can you tell a jaguar's spots from a leopard's? | A jaguar's rosettes usually have small dots inside | A leopard has stripes on its back and a jaguar has spots | A jaguar has no spots at all | A leopard's spots are bigger and blue
exercise: What is a black panther? | A dark leopard or jaguar with spots that still show | A separate kind of black cat from Africa | A black lion without a mane | A puma that has rolled in mud
exercise: How can a clouded leopard come down a tree head first? | Its ankles turn backwards so it can grip the bark | It has sticky pads on its toes, like a gecko's | It slides down on its belly | It uses its teeth to hold the bark
exercise: Why does a cheetah sit up on a mound? | It hunts by sight and looks out over open land | It is scared of the ground | It is trying to get closer to the sun to warm up its spotted coat | It wants to roar at the visitors
exercise: Why can't a cheetah run at top speed for long? | It overheats after a few hundred metres | Its legs are far too short and thin to keep going | It forgets where it is going | Its spots slow it down
exercise: What is enrichment in a zoo? | Giving animals things to search, smell and solve | Giving animals extra money for food | Making the enclosure smaller and safer for them | Teaching animals to do clever tricks for the visitors
exercise: Why does a keeper spray perfume on a log for the lions? | New smells give the cats something to explore | To cover up the smell of all the visitors | To make the lions smell nicer for photos | To keep the biting flies off the lions
exercise: What sounds does a puma make? | Chirps, hisses, purrs and screams, but no roar | Only loud roars, just like a lion | Barks like a dog | It is completely silent, all its life long
exercise: Is a mountain lion a lion? | No, it is another name for the puma | Yes, a lion of the mountains | Yes, a lion with no mane | No, it is a kind of bear
exercise: Roughly how many kinds of wild cats are there? | About 40 | About 4 or so | About 400 | About 4,000
exercise: How does the sand cat cope with hot desert sand? | Hairy paws stop its feet from burning | It only walks on the rocks and never on sand | It wears its claws out on purpose | It never leaves its burrow
exercise: Why does a young tiger play with a ball? | Play trains the chasing and pouncing it needs to hunt | It has nothing at all to learn | Balls taste like meat to tigers | Keepers punish tigers that do not play with the toys they are given
test: «my cat goes mad for a cardboard box, would a tiger care?» What is right? | Yes, big cats love boxes, and zoos give them as enrichment | No, tigers are scared of cardboard because it rustles and smells odd | No, only kittens play with boxes | Tigers eat the box and nothing else
test: In a cartoon a huge black panther is «a different animal from leopards». What would you fix? | It is a dark leopard or jaguar, with spots in bright light | It is a black lion from the mountains | It is right, panthers are their own species | It is a puma that rolled in mud
test: You are a keeper. A tiger keeps pacing. Which idea would help most? | Hide food in boxes and change the enclosure | Give it the same bowl at the same time | Make the enclosure smaller | Keep visitors away and keep the lights off all day long
test: 2 snow leopards, a jaguar and 3 cheetahs. how many tails | 6 | 4 | 24 | 3
test: «jaguar and leopard, same thing innit» What is right? | No, jaguars have dots in their rosettes and live in the Americas | Yes, jaguar is just the American word for leopard, mate | No, jaguars have stripes | Yes, they live together in the same forests of Africa and Asia, side by side
test: What would happen if a cheetah tried to chase a gazelle for a whole kilometre at top speed? | It would overheat and have to stop | It would just keep getting faster | It would turn into a leopard | Nothing, cheetahs never tire
test: A friend says the puma is a small lion because it is called a mountain lion. What is right? | It is a puma, a cat that purrs and cannot roar | Yes, it is a young female lion that has left its pride | Yes, it is a lion from American mountains | It is a big kind of house cat that roars
test: A cartoon has a cat that whistles and chirps like a bird. Which real cat comes closest? | The puma | The African lion | The tiger | The jaguar
test: Why would a fishing cat have slightly webbed feet? | It hunts in swamps and water | It lives on hot desert sand | It climbs tall trees in the forest | It walks on deep snow
test: «why does a turtle eater need such a strong bite» Which answer fits? | It has to crack a hard shell to reach the meat | Turtles are very fast and must be grabbed | Turtles bite back very hard, so it must bite first | Turtle meat is tougher than any bone
test: 4 sand cats and 1 fishing cat in the small cats corner. how many paws | 20 | 16 | 10 | 25
exam: Identify the principal function of the broad, fur-covered paws of the snow leopard. | To distribute its weight upon the snow as snowshoes do | To permit rapid swimming in mountain rivers | To enable it to descend trees head first in pursuit of prey | To amplify the sound of its roar
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of enrichment in zoological collections? | The provision of objects to search, smell and solve | The reduction of enclosure size in the interest of safety | The instruction of animals in tricks for public display | The provision of supplementary food on public holidays
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the animal commonly designated a «black panther». | It is a dark leopard or jaguar whose rosettes remain visible | It constitutes a separate species native to South Asia | It is a lion lacking a mane | It is the offspring of a puma and a jaguar, bred in captivity for its colour
exam: Ascertain the approximate number of species of wild cat presently recognised. | About forty | About four in all | About four hundred | About four thousand
